Fix heap over-read in pinyin_get_full_pinyin_auxiliary_text.

`len = cursor - begin` is a raw-input byte delta. When the canonical
pinyin string is shorter than the raw input (SECONDARY_ZHUYIN, and any
scheme where raw-key length exceeds pinyin byte length), `len` can
exceed `strlen(pinyin)`; `g_strdup(pinyin + len)` then reads past the
`NUL` into adjacent heap and injects garbage bytes into the returned
aux text. Clamp the offset at the call site — one line, tightest fix,
and `pinyin` is in scope only there (the natural hoist that would let
the clamp live on the `len =` line would be a two-line change).

`g_strndup(pinyin, len)` at the previous line is already safe: GLib's
implementation uses `strncpy`, which stops at the first NUL, so no
over-read is possible there.

See docs/findings/full-pinyin-aux-overread.md for the reproduction and
the venue survey. Applied against libpinyin 2.11.91 @
`0c5e80e1200f84fab185d1c5bde458b770a0636c`.

--- a/src/pinyin.cpp
+++ b/src/pinyin.cpp
@@ -3415,7 +3415,7 @@ bool pinyin_get_full_pinyin_auxiliary_text(pinyin_instance_t * instance,
         if (begin < cursor && cursor < end) {
             gchar * pinyin = key.get_pinyin_string();
             gchar * left = g_strndup(pinyin, len);
-            gchar * right = g_strdup(pinyin + len);
+            gchar * right = g_strdup(pinyin + std_lite::min((size_t)len, strlen(pinyin)));
             middle = g_strconcat(left, "|", right, " ", NULL);
             g_free(left);
             g_free(right);
